Z||000000||WKDFIRO||11-17-1999||03:29 PM||||I caught a rock in one of my headlights and need to change it out but I am intrigued by these "cool blue" lights you were talking about. I am happy with my lights now but I do like those projector lights on the new cars. How much do they cost, where and how do you like them?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||11-17-1999||08:20 PM||||My eyes are a bit over-sensitive to light and I find those blue lights quite annoying...||||reg||9
Z||000002||Tim||11-17-1999||08:35 PM||||I've driven my son's-in-law Z-24 convertible with the blue lights.  His car is teal and they look good from the outside.  I didn't think they aided the night time vision any, of course my illegal 90/130 watt H-4's helps me see well.  The blue lights(fake HID) look nice, but H-4 is cheaper, more available and better performance.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cooter||11-17-1999||08:49 PM||||I just pulled the blue beams out of my Civic and threw them in the trash. THey looked neat at first, but on a long trip, they caused a big time head-ache.||||reg||13
Z||000004||88 Fiero||11-17-1999||10:24 PM||||I am not an avid fan of the blue lights...one thing for sure is that it's illegal in California...that's only for offroad use...but I notice that there are alot of import cars that have the "Super white" bulb and I love those...I've seen it on an old Lexus 400 and it looks great...I'll find out if there is one available with the H4 headlight set-up co'z I got one on my fiero... I know I will order for my MX-6 Mazda Coupe....||||reg||1
Z||000005||WKDFIRO||11-17-1999||11:51 PM||||I thought I saw an advertisement for DOT approved blue lights at Pep Boys while I was in for something else. If blue lights are illegal I doubt that Mercedes and Lincolns would be on the road. If it is TOO blue I can see a problem with actually looking at the road as opposed to looking cool. I would like to know how much they are. I wouldn't mind being the guinea pig for such an experiment if the price is right.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Racer 1||11-18-1999||02:54 AM||||THe "super white" looking ones are called Hyper White bulbs. THe cool blue and hyper whites can be picked up at Autozone.Cool blue ones are 2 for $19.98<p>[This message has been edited by Racer 1 (edited 11-18-1999).]||||reg||10
Z||000007||fierospeeder||11-18-1999||03:23 AM||||i seen those mercedes hope thats how you spell that, i just know fiero<BR>anyway those come stock with the blue light and they are suppose to improve visibility.  You can see someone driving with the blue lights from oncoming traffic on the highway.<BR>Driving at night with my fiero was the worst, i just hate driving at night at all.  Having a tall vehicle behind my fiero having their lights going into the cabin is just frustrating and those SUVs seem like they have brights on.  Theres times that its hard to see the road because someone has brights on and your being blinded by that.  Anyone align their headlights anymore?  uh oh better stop im bashing lights now ||||reg||1
Z||000008||batboy||11-18-1999||03:21 PM||||Since we started bashing lights...my pet peave is all those "too cool for school" young dudes that always drive at night with their auxilary driving lights on and won't turn them off for oncoming traffic. For a while I tried flashing my brights for a second to give them a hint that they were half blinding me, but usually they just flip on their high beams and then totally blind me. Now, if I'm driving my Suburban, I take the covers off my KC daylighters...heh, heh, heh. When they flip on their brights because I flashed them, I hit my brights and off road lights both. They usually turn off their driving lights for me then.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Cooter||11-18-1999||03:29 PM||||That is funny because I do the same thing with my Suburban! I have two sets of Daylighters hooked up to come on with the high beams on the truck. Nobody ever keeps their brights on for long when they get hit with 600 watts of Halogen to the eyes.||||reg||14
Z||000010||Racer 1||11-18-1999||04:35 PM||||Police here will give you a ticket if you run your Aux driving lights on within 100 yards of oncoming traffic. In my metro I just use my hand held spotlight 600,000 candle power that way I can shine the light right at there car. ||||reg||6
Z||000011||theogre||11-18-1999||10:40 PM||||I don't know if DOT has aproved them for the states or not. Seems to me they did. In which case some of the new lighting systems you are seeing on the road are or will be using "gas discharge" lamps. Imagine a photo flash that goes on forever, that's how these work. No halogen lamp is as bright as these in the same space or current draw.<P>The rest of the beam projectors use the back loaded halogen lamps same as those body contour jobs do.<P>Driving lights are mainly a problem becuase hardly anyone who has them has the things aimed right. but then that's what's wrong with half the cars on the road anyway with the main lamps as well. Focused lamps just light where they aim. If that's up into a tree they don't know the diferance.<P>Here if they see you with the covers off your "off road" lights they will ticket you. the lights don't have to even be on. The lights can even be electrically disconnected and you will still get the ticket.||||reg||1
